# What does NOT ship in the .vsix. Without this file the package contained 157
# files and 334 KB around a 5 KB bundle — including the whole template
# submodule, which is a separate product with its own releases and has no
# business inside an editor extension.
#
# Everything the extension needs at runtime is the bundle; the sources are in
# the repository, and that is where anybody debugging it should be reading them.
# tools/vsix.test.ts holds this true.

# The image's content travels in the package: the extension composes a
# project's Dockerfile from it, so a project needs nothing but the extension
# installed. 57 files, 132 KB.
#
# **Only the tests are excluded, and there is no negation here on purpose.**
# Excluding the directories and re-admitting them with `!core/cont-init/**`
# was tried and shipped `40-ai-memory.test.sh`: later patterns win, so a
# negation re-admits everything under its path including what an earlier line
# removed. Naming only what must not ship leaves no ordering to get wrong —
# and a stack added to `stacks/` travels without anybody editing this file.
#
# `scripts/` never ships at all: CI's guards have no business inside an editor
# extension.
# This repository's own CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md are its instructions, not a
# project's: the first imports MODES.md and docs/RULES.md from paths that exist
# in no other tree, and an import resolving to nothing says nothing. What a
# project receives is assets/project/, which tools/vsix.test.ts *requires*
# rather than permits — AGENTS.md shipped in one person's package and nobody
# else's for exactly as long as it was only permitted.
CLAUDE.md
AGENTS.md
SECURITY.md

core/**/*.test.sh
stacks/**/*.test.sh
scripts/**

# Agent skills for working *on* this repository, not for anyone using the
# extension. Never tracked in git, so a CI package built from actions/checkout
# never saw them and the published .vsix is clean — but `npm run package` on a
# developer's machine did ship all sixteen, which makes local packaging differ
# from the pipeline's. That exact class of defect cost this repository a
# misdiagnosis once already, in the fix titled "packaging builds what it
# packages".
.claude/**
.agents/**

.github/**
# docs/agent/ ships: core/Dockerfile.frag copies it into the image, and the
# build context of a real build is this package. Excluding docs/** whole kept
# it out from 0d3dd5f on, and every CI job stayed green, because CI builds
# from a checkout. The rest of docs/ is this repository's own planning. A new
# directory under docs/ will ship until it is named here, and the allowlist
# in tools/vsix.test.ts will refuse it.
docs/*.md
docs/DEBTS/**
docs/PLANNING/**
docs/srs/**
docs/**/*.test.sh
src/**
tools/**
node_modules/**
dist/**/*.map

.ai-memory.toml
# This machine's answers, under both names: the current one and the one a
# project may still carry until it is adopted. Only the old name was listed
# here after the rename, so a local package shipped the current manifest.
.agent-container.stack.json
.code-server.stack.json

# The generated dev container configuration, which is this machine's answers:
# absolute paths to the project and to the person's own ~/.claude, the core
# count, the memory, the devices this host happens to have. The same class as
# the skills above — never tracked in git, so a CI package from
# actions/checkout never saw it and the published .vsix is clean, while
# `npm run package` after opening the project once ships somebody's home
# directory inside an editor extension. Caught by the allowlist in
# tools/vsix.test.ts, which is what that allowlist exists for.
.devcontainer/**
.gitattributes
.gitignore
.gitmodules
.release-please-manifest.json
.vscodeignore
release-please-config.json
tsconfig.json
package-lock.json
CLAUDE.md
*.vsix
